<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aznar Galíndez I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 839</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Aznar Galíndez I was the first known count of Aragon, ruling the small county around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaca">Jaca</a> in the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a> in the early 9th century under Frankish protection. Around 820 he was driven out by his son-in-law García the Bad, who was allied with the rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamplona">Pamplona</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Carolingians</a> gave him the counties of Urgell and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cerdanya">Cerdanya</a> instead. His son Galindo later recovered Aragon.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aznar-galindez-i.jpg" alt="Aznar Galíndez I" width="320" height="322" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Autor desconhecido · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aznar_I_Gal%C3%ADndez.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Aragon took its name from a river in the central Pyrenees, where a small county around Jaca appeared in the early ninth century under Frankish influence, ruled by counts such as <b>Aznar Galíndez</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-aragon">Kingdom of Aragon · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Its first known count, <b>Aznar Galíndez I</b>, ruled in the 800s under Carolingian protection before being expelled by rivals allied with Pamplona.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-aragon">County of Aragon</a></li></ul></section>
